# The forge host observes itself (49a8d842). The estate loop watched
# boss-gcp only, while the forge — Forgejo, the registry, the CI
# runner, and the tightest disk in the estate (228G, 83% full on
# 2026-08-30) — produced no observations at all: its disk could fill
# and the comparison would say nothing, which is how 2026-09-02's fill
# blocked every train unseen. boss-estate-observe-host.service in
# infra/estate/ is the same observer for boss-gcp, with its own note
# that the forge "runs the SAME pair with two overrides, installed by
# hand until its config-not-in-repo debt is paid". This is that debt
# paid: the forge's pair lives in the tree, in install.sh's UNITS, and
# forge-converge installs it like every other unit here.
#
# Same script, two overrides: HOST_ID=forge (must match the estate
# node row id — a wrong id is "never observed" for the box that
# matters) and the forge's checkout path. The wrap pair is the
# timers-leave-a-packet contract; the label names the host so the
# daily packet reads apart from boss-gcp's.
[Unit]
Description=BOSS forge: observe this host and record what was there
Wants=network-online.target
After=network-online.target

[Service]
EnvironmentFile=/etc/boss/sor.env
Type=oneshot
# A oneshot's start timeout is infinity by default. The nodefs line below
# calls the cluster's API server and each node's Talos API through
# docker; a stalled daemon or API would hold this unit activating
# forever, the timer could not start it again, and the forge's OWN host
# reading — the one the boarding check reads — would stop with it
# (review of 4327d1a3, finding 3). Ten minutes is far past a healthy run
# (the host reading takes seconds; five nodes' Talos calls are bounded
# at 20s each inside their containers).
TimeoutStartSec=10min
# ROOT SINCE 2026-10-06 (design-doc 20058482, question forge-token;
# backlog 88379df3). It ran as david because nothing here needs root —
# df/nproc/meminfo and a POST — and that is still true of the reading.
# What changed is the estate machine token: forge-converge deposits it
# root-only at /etc/boss/machine-token, because a car's recorded probe
# runs on this host as david and must not be able to read it, and this
# unit's wrap pair and its posts present it. Of this host's four units
# that ran as david this is the one that needs nothing of david's — no
# git in the owner's checkout, no rootless docker daemon, no state under
# $HOME — and boss-gcp's twin (infra/estate/boss-estate-observe-host.service)
# has always run as root. The other three keep their user and stay
# unstamped; machine_token_deposit_sh.rs names each and why.
# The credential doors need no change: `sudo -n docker run --mount`
# (ops-credentials.sh) answers root as it answered david, and the host
# reading's credential check stats /etc/boss-ops directly, as the
# converge's does.
User=root
Environment=HOST_ID=forge
# THE SPOOL IS ROOT'S OWN DIRECTORY. observe-lib.sh's default,
# /var/tmp/boss-estate-spool, was made on this host by this unit while
# it ran as david, and its replay `cat`s every file there and POSTs it:
# root replaying out of a directory david can write would send whatever
# a link planted there points at (the shape review A1 of 5ef6db0b found
# in forge-converge). /var/lib/boss is root:root 0755, held so on every
# converge tick (converge-hold.sh prepare). A reading still waiting in
# the old directory is not replayed; the series shows that gap.
Environment=SPOOL_DIR=/var/lib/boss/estate-spool
# WHAT THE MOVE LEAVES BEHIND, said rather than found: up to 200 readings
# (SPOOL_MAX) still waiting in david's old spool are never replayed, and
# david's unrecorded-runs ledger for this kind
# (~david/.boss-maintenance-unrecorded) is orphaned — root keeps its own
# from here on (infra/boss-maintenance-wrap.sh).
#
# THE TOKEN'S HEADER FILE NEVER OUTLIVES A RUN (adversarial review
# 9a1e289b, F7). As root this unit's wrap pair presents the machine
# token, and once its posts are stamped they will too; the 0600 header
# file infra/lib/secret-header.sh hands curl is made in
# $RUNTIME_DIRECTORY when a unit declares one — tmpfs under /run, 0700,
# removed by systemd when the oneshot stops whatever the exit, a SIGKILL
# included — and under the host's shared /tmp otherwise. LimitCORE=0: no
# curl of this run dumps a core holding it. The shape of
# forge-converge.service (359a811c); RuntimeDirectory= makes no mount
# namespace.
RuntimeDirectory=estate-observe-host
RuntimeDirectoryMode=0700
LimitCORE=0
# BOSS_JOBS_URL and JOBS_API come from /etc/boss/sor.env (EnvironmentFile=
# above), rendered by install.sh from infra/estate/estate.toml; the
# helpers refuse without one, and every Exec line reads the same file.
ExecStartPre=-/var/lib/boss/tree/current/infra/boss-maintenance-wrap.sh maintenance-estate-observe-host "estate host observation (forge)"
ExecStart=/var/lib/boss/tree/current/infra/estate/observe-host.sh
# Each CLUSTER node's kubelet filesystem, read here through the Talos API
# with the talosconfig this host holds (backlog eeac3d56): the in-cluster
# observer used to read it through the kubelet as `get nodes/proxy`,
# which is exec into any pod, and now carries this reading across
# instead. SECOND, so the forge's own reading — the one the boarding
# check reads — never waits on five nodes' Talos calls, and `-` so a
# failed read never fails this unit: an unread node is recorded as
# unread, and a reading that stops arriving ages out into
# `disk_unmeasured` on the kubernetes-nodes observation, where the
# blind-class alarm reads it. systemd runs no later line after a failed
# one, so while the host observation cannot post (the record is dark)
# this is not read either — and it could not have posted.
ExecStart=-/var/lib/boss/tree/current/infra/estate/observe-nodefs.sh
# Every instance volume's filesystem — each PersistentVolumeClaim in the
# instance namespaces, capacity and free, from the kubelet's volume
# stats through the admin kubeconfig (backlog 21ee3b4e, incident
# d3c0a67c: the system of record's Postgres volume filled and nothing
# was watching). THIRD and `-` for the nodefs line's reasons: the
# forge's own reading never waits on it, and a claim that cannot be read
# is recorded unread, not a failed unit. Five nodes' summaries are
# bounded at 20s each, inside the ten minutes above.
ExecStart=-/var/lib/boss/tree/current/infra/estate/observe-volumes.sh
ExecStopPost=-/var/lib/boss/tree/current/infra/boss-step.sh maintenance-estate-observe-host run
